-- APPLY-ONE, the THIRD outcome — c0 §43 `law.brace`:
--
--     "subject is a DESCRIPTOR -> descriptor application"
--     "subject is a CALLABLE   -> ordinary application of a structured pack"
--     "subject is NEITHER      -> diagnostic, never a guess"
--
-- This is the NEITHER row, and it is a rejection fixture rather than a value
-- fixture for the reason the law states in its own `fails` clause: the defect
-- being pinned is "resolving the shared form as a call without asking what the
-- name is". A guess has no value to compare against — it has an ANSWER, and the
-- answer is wrong. So the pin has to be the diagnostic.
--
-- The negative twin of `examples/apply_one_pack_proof.id`. That file's rows
-- all have a subject that resolves; this one has a subject that resolves to
-- nothing, and the two must be read together. Either alone passes under a
-- compiler that had simply picked one reading for every brace.
--
-- MEASURED 2026-08-09, after gap[092]:
--
--     error: 'nosuch' is neither a descriptor nor a callable, so the braced
--            application has no subject: descriptor space holds no 'nosuch'
--            and callable space holds no 'nosuch' (no declaration, no builtin,
--            no foreign import)
--
-- It used to read `call to undeclared function 'nosuch'` — the right REFUSAL in
-- the wrong SENTENCE. That was the call path narrating its own unconditional
-- win, the exact framing §43 deletes, and it named only ONE of the two homes
-- the resolution rule consults. The sentence now names both, says which FACE
-- was written, and says what each home was asked for. The refusal itself is
-- unchanged, because it was never the refusal that was wrong.
--
-- Still pinned on the substring `nosuch`, which survived the rewording exactly
-- as this file predicted it would.

nosuch{ x = 1, y = 2 }
